// An intermediate inside one expression is in no local and no argument array,
// so the shadow stack could not see it.  That was survivable while a
// collection happened only where a program asked for one -- an expression is
// never interrupted by its own statement boundary -- and stopped being
// survivable the moment collection became AUTOMATIC, because a safe point
// inside a CALLEE fires while the caller is part way through an expression.
//
// ⚠️ AN EXPLICIT `Collect ()` CANNOT CATCH THIS, which is what makes the case
// worth reading.  A call written as a statement is a statement boundary: no
// expression is in flight, so every live value is a local and the shadow
// stack sees all of them.  The collection has to arrive from somewhere the
// program did not ask for it, which is why this leans on `Spelled` being long
// enough to trip the automatic safe point by itself.
//
// ⚠️ THE OPERAND ORDER IS THE WHOLE CASE.  C does not say which operand of a
// `+` is evaluated first, so this gives BOTH ends something to lose: a call
// that allocates sits on either side of a call that loops.  Whichever end the
// compiler evaluates first is the end at risk.
//
// It failed as a tail of 'abcd' becoming a free-list pointer read as text,
// which is what a swept-and-reused payload looks like.

function Spelled (S : String, Times : Integer) : String;
var
    Out : String;

begin
    Out := '';

    // Long on purpose: the safe point is at the top of a loop body, so the
    // iterations here are what bring a collection on while the CALLER's
    // expression is half built.
    for var Pass : Integer := 0; Pass < Times; Pass := Pass + 1 do
        for var I : Integer := 0; I < Length (S); I := I + 1 do
            Out := Str (S[I]);

    Exit Out + S;
end

begin
    var Last := '';

    for var N : Integer := 0; N < 400; N := N + 1 do
        Last := Spelled ('abcd', 300) + Str (N) + Spelled ('efgh', 300);

    WriteLn ('last: ' + Str (Last));
end
